<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Richard the Justiciar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 858–921</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Richard the Justiciar was count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/autun">Autun</a> and the first duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>. A brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boso-of-provence">Boso of Provence</a>, he gathered the counties of western Burgundy under his rule and defended them against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a>, defeating them at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-florentin">Saint-Florentin</a> in 898. He was recognised as duke by around 918. His son Raoul became king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-francia">West Francia</a> in 923.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/richard-the-justiciar.jpg" alt="Richard the Justiciar" width="320" height="267" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown artist, c. 1275–1280 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fuite_des_Normands_devant_l%27arm%C3%A9e_de_Richard,_duc_de_Bourgogne.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Richard was a Bosonid, the son of Bivin of Gorze and Richildis. His elder brother was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boso-of-provence">Boso of Provence</a>, and his younger sister Richildis became the second wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bald">Charles the Bald</a>. In 875, after the death of the Emperor Louis II, Richard and Boso accompanied Charles to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> for his imperial coronation. In February 876, at Pavia, Charles named Boso Duke and Viceroy of Italy and Duke of Provence. When Charles died in 877, Boso returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and left the government of Italy and Provence to Richard and Hugh the Abbot as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/missi-dominici">missi dominici</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 879 Boso declared himself King of Provence after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-stammerer">Louis the Stammerer</a>, but Richard broke with him and took his county of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/autun">Autun</a>, which Carloman II confirmed to him in 880. The two brothers fought on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saone">Saône</a>, and Richard captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macon">Mâcon</a> and garrisoned it in the name of Carloman and Louis III under Bernard Plantapilosa. After taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a> he besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienne">Vienne</a>, Boso's capital, where Louis, Carloman and the Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-fat">Charles the Fat</a> joined him. In 882 he drove Boso out and sent Boso's wife Ermengard and their children Engelberga and Louis to Autun as prisoners.</p><p>After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-fat">Charles the Fat</a> in 888, Richard supported the claim of Duke Rudolph to be King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-burgundy">Upper Burgundy</a> and married Rudolph's sister Adelaide, daughter of Conrad II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auxerre">Auxerre</a>. In 890 he also supported the coronation of his nephew Louis as King of Provence. He gained suzerainty over all the counties of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> except <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macon">Mâcon</a>. He was called dux by 890 and marchio by 900, and by 918 dux Burgundionem or dux Burgundiae, a title that probably meant feudal suzerainty over many counties of one region rather than a unified duchy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Richard died in 921 and was buried at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sens">Sens</a>. At his deathbed a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> urged him to ask pardon for the blood he had shed. He answered that when he killed a brigand he had saved the lives of honest men, since the death of one prevented his accomplices from doing more evil. By <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adelaide">Adelaide</a> he had several sons and daughters, among them Rudolph, his successor and later King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francia">Francia</a>, and Hugh the Black, later Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>. His daughters married Gilbert, Duke of Burgundy, Reginar II, Count of Hainaut, and Litaud I, Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macon">Mâcon</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Richard the Justiciar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its founder was <b>Richard the Justiciar</b>, count of Autun and brother of Boso of Provence, who gathered several counties under his control, defeated a Viking army near Saint-Florentin in 898 and was recognised as the leading prince of the region, with the ducal title attested by around 918.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-burgundy">Duchy of Burgundy · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auxerre">Auxerre</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.8,3.57&amp;year=898" aria-label="Auxerre on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Richard the Justiciar"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5205 6030h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5205 6030h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=47.8,3.57&amp;year=898">What was here in 898 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
